-- ---------- examples -----------------------------------------------------
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (1,1,$oq2$Evaluate 999 999 × 222 222 + 333 333 × 333 334.$oq2$,$oq2$Rewrite 999 999 × 222 222 as 333 333 × 3 × 222 222 = 333 333 × 666 666. Now both terms contain 333 333: 333 333 × (666 666 + 333 334) = 333 333 × 1 000 000 = 333 333 000 000.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (1,2,$oq2$Find the sum of the digits of 333…3 (2008 threes) × 666…6 (2008 sixes).$oq2$,$oq2$Turn it into 999…9 × 222…2 = (10^2008 − 1) × 222…2 = 222…2 000…0 − 222…2. The subtraction gives 222…21777…78 with 2007 twos, a 1, 2007 sevens and an 8. Pair a 2 with a 7 (= 9) and the 1 with the 8 (= 9): 2008 pairs of 9, so the digit sum is 2008 × 9 = 18 072.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (1,3,$oq2$A three-digit number has digit sum 21; its ones digit is bigger than its tens digit; swapping the hundreds and ones digits makes it 198 bigger. Find it.$oq2$,$oq2$If the number is abc, swapping gives cba, and cba − abc = 99(c − a) = 198, so c − a = 2. Now search digit triples with a + b + c = 21, c = a + 2 and c > b: 678 and 957 both work.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (1,4,$oq2$Miss Cussler was born on 1 January. In 2002 her age equalled the sum of the digits of her birth year. How old was she?$oq2$,$oq2$Let the year be 1abc. Then 2002 − (1000 + 100a + 10b + c) = 1 + a + b + c, i.e. 1001 = 101a + 11b + 2c. Since 11b + 2c ≤ 117, a must be 9, leaving 92 = 11b + 2c → b = 8, c = 2. Born 1982, so she was 20.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (2,1,$oq2$5 tables and 18 chairs cost $594; a table costs as much as 3 chairs.$oq2$,$oq2$Replace 5 tables by 15 chairs: 33 chairs = $594 → chair $18, table $54.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (2,2,$oq2$4 basketballs and 6 footballs cost $460; a basketball is $15 more than a football.$oq2$,$oq2$Turn basketballs into footballs: 460 − 4×15 = 400 for 10 footballs → football $40, basketball $55. (Or turn footballs into basketballs: 460 + 6×15 = 550 ÷ 10.)$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (2,3,$oq2$5 kg sugar + 6 kg rice = $84.50; 2 kg sugar + 3 kg rice = $41.$oq2$,$oq2$Double the second: 4 sugar + 6 rice = $82. Subtract from the first: 1 kg sugar = $2.50; rice $12/kg.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (2,4,$oq2$10 rabbits = 2 goats, 9 goats = 3 cows, 8 cows = 2 horses. How many rabbits equal 5 horses?$oq2$,$oq2$5 horses → 20 cows → 60 goats → 300 rabbits.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (2,5,$oq2$Running 1000 m and walking 2000 m takes 25 min; running 4000 m and walking 500 m also takes 25 min. Running speed?$oq2$,$oq2$Scale the second by 4 (16 000 r + 2000 w = 100 min) and subtract the first: 15 000 m of running = 75 min → 200 m/min.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (3,1,$oq2$AB + AB + AB = CCC.$oq2$,$oq2$CCC = 111C must be divisible by 3: C = 1 → 37 (A=3, B=7); C = 2 → 74 (A=7, B=4). Larger C gives three digits, so two solutions.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (3,2,$oq2$(a) ABC × A = 10AC. (b) ABCD × 9 = DCBA.$oq2$,$oq2$(a) A × A must be 9 plus a carry of 1 → A = 3, and C × 3 ends in C → C = 5; then B = 4: 345 × 3 = 1035. (b) The product has 4 digits so A = 1, hence D = 9; no carry into the thousands forces B = 0 and then C = 8: 1089 × 9 = 9801.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (3,3,$oq2$(a) CD + BCD + BACD = 1989. (b) C + CAC + ACBC = 2AAC.$oq2$,$oq2$(a) Ones: 3D ends in 9 → D = 3; tens: 3C + 0 ends in 8 → C = 6 with carry 1; then A = 7, B = 1 (63 + 163 + 1763 = 1989). (b) 4C ends in C → C = 5 (carry 2); A = 1; B = 9: 5 + 515 + 1595 = 2115.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (3,4,$oq2$Alice's house number abcd becomes bcda when the first digit moves to the end, and the new number is 4707 bigger.$oq2$,$oq2$1000b + 100c + 10d + a − (1000a + 100b + 10c + d) = 4707 → 900b + 90c + 9d − 999a = 4707 → 100b + 10c + d − 111a = 523. Try a = 1: 100b + 10c + d = 634 → 1634 (check: 6341 − 1634 = 4707).$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (4,1,$oq2$40 students; 30 did the maths homework and 25 the science homework. How many did both?$oq2$,$oq2$30 + 25 − 40 = 15.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (4,2,$oq2$Class of 42: 22 scored ≥ 90 in maths, 30 in science. Both?$oq2$,$oq2$22 + 30 − 42 = 10.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (4,3,$oq2$How many of 1–100 are neither multiples of 3 nor divisible by 7?$oq2$,$oq2$33 multiples of 3, 14 of 7, 4 of 21. 100 − (33 + 14 − 4) = 57.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (4,4,$oq2$48 students; 28 in Robotics, 14 in Speech & Drama, 10 in both. Neither?$oq2$,$oq2$48 − (28 + 14 − 10) = 16.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (4,5,$oq2$Three identical circles of area 157 cm² overlap; the figure covers 280 cm²; the triple overlap is 56 cm². Find S₁ + S₂ + S₃.$oq2$,$oq2$471 − (S₁ + S₂ + S₃) + 56 = 280 → wait, careful: each pairwise overlap region as measured in the book excludes the centre, so 471 − (S₁+56) − (S₂+56) − (S₃+56) + 56 = 280 → S₁ + S₂ + S₃ = 79 cm².$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (5,1,$oq2$A pair of adult rabbits produces a pair of babies each month; babies take a month to mature. Pairs after 12 months?$oq2$,$oq2$Tree of A (adult) / B (baby): 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, … → month 12 gives 233 pairs.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (5,2,$oq2$Ways to climb 6 steps taking 1 or 2 at a time.$oq2$,$oq2$Ways to reach step n = ways to n−1 + ways to n−2: 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (5,3,$oq2$5, a, 5+a, … (Fibonacci rule); the 6th term is 50. Find a.$oq2$,$oq2$Terms: 5, a, 5+a, 5+2a, 10+3a, 15+5a = 50 → a = 7.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (5,4,$oq2$Jolene saves $a in January and $b in February, then each month the sum of the previous two. In June she saves $75. Find a, b (whole numbers).$oq2$,$oq2$June = 3a + 5b = 75 → a = 5, b = 12 (the only positive whole-number solution with a a multiple of 5 and b < 15 that works: 15 + 60).$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (6,1,$oq2$Evaluate: ^5P_3, ^30P_4, ^5C_3, ^10P_4, ^6C_4, 3×^4P_1 − ^3P_2, ^10C_3×^6C_2, ^8P_4×^5P_4.$oq2$,$oq2$60; 657 720; 10; 5040; 15; 12 − 6 = 6; 120 × 15 = 1800; 1680 × 120 = 201 600.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (6,2,$oq2$Choose 6 of 12 volleyball players.$oq2$,$oq2$^12C_6 = 924.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (6,3,$oq2$Three-digit numbers with distinct digits from 1–5; how many, and how many are odd?$oq2$,$oq2$^5P_3 = 60; odd: ones digit 3 choices, then 4 × 3 → 36.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (6,4,$oq2$An ant walks home along arrows through a grid via point C.$oq2$,$oq2$3 routes A→C and 3 routes C→home → 3 × 3 = 9.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (6,5,$oq2$Line a has 5 points, line b has 4. Triangles and quadrilaterals with these vertices?$oq2$,$oq2$Triangles: ^5C_2·4 + ^4C_2·5 = 40 + 30 = 70. Quadrilaterals: ^5C_2·^4C_2 = 60.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (6,6,$oq2$Four-digit numbers starting with 1 with exactly two identical digits.$oq2$,$oq2$Case 1: the pair is two 1s — place the second 1 (3 ways), fill the other two slots with distinct non-1 digits (9 × 8): 216. Case 2: the pair is some other digit (9 choices), placed in 3 ways, remaining slot 8 digits: 216. Total 432.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (8,1,$oq2$A train takes 65 s to cross a 1200 m bridge and 85 s to cross a 1600 m tunnel. Speed and length?$oq2$,$oq2$Extra 400 m took 20 s → 20 m/s. Length = 20 × 65 − 1200 = 100 m.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (8,2,$oq2$Out at 96 km/h, back at 72 km/h. Average speed?$oq2$,$oq2$Assume 96 km each way: 1 h + 1⅓ h = 7/3 h for 192 km → 82 2/7 km/h.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (8,3,$oq2$At 50 km/h a scooter arrives at 11 am, at 75 km/h at 10 am. Speed to arrive at 10.30?$oq2$,$oq2$Let the 75 km/h trip take n hours: 50(n+1) = 75n → n = 2, distance 150 km. 150 ÷ 2.5 = 60 km/h.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (8,4,$oq2$Frank climbs 2 steps/s and reaches the top of a moving escalator in 25 s; Ronnie climbs 3 steps per 2 s and takes 30 s. Steps when stationary?$oq2$,$oq2$50 + 25n = 45 + 30n → n = 1 → 75 steps.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (8,5,$oq2$Cars at 62 and 54 km/h leave A for B; the faster car meets an oncoming truck after 10 h and the slower one meets it 1 h later. Truck's speed?$oq2$,$oq2$After 10 h the cars are 80 km apart; the slower car and truck close that in 1 h: 54 + t = 80 → 26 km/h.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (8,6,$oq2$A and B are 420 km apart. If A starts 2.5 h earlier they meet 2.5 h after B starts; if B starts 2 h earlier they meet 3 h after A starts. Speeds?$oq2$,$oq2$5a + 2.5b = 420 and 3a + 5b = 420 → a = 60, b = 48 km/h.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (8,7,$oq2$Cars from A and B with speeds 5:4 meet; afterwards the first slows by 1/5 and the second speeds up 20%. When the first reaches B the second is still 25 km from A. Distance AB?$oq2$,$oq2$Split AB into 9 units; after meeting the first car has 4 units left at speed 4 (1 h). In that hour the second covers 24/5 of its 5 units, short by 1/5 unit = 25 km → unit = 125 → 1125 km.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (9,1,$oq2$Smallest n so that 2008 + n is divisible by 13 and 19.$oq2$,$oq2$13 × 19 = 247; 247 × 9 = 2223 → n = 215.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (9,2,$oq2$Smallest n making 693 × n a perfect square.$oq2$,$oq2$693 = 3² × 7 × 11 → n = 77.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (9,3,$oq2$All numbers 568abc divisible by 3, 4 and 5.$oq2$,$oq2$c = 0, b even, 19 + a + b divisible by 3 → 17 numbers: 568020, 568050, 568080, 568140, 568170 … (the book tabulates all 17).$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (9,4,$oq2$Is 3ab written 100 times always a multiple of 91?$oq2$,$oq2$Pair up: 3ab3ab = 3ab × 1001 = 3ab × 11 × 91. Fifty such pairs → yes, for every a, b.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
