insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (13,5,$oq2$A square is divided into four regions with areas a, b, c, d where a = b, a + b + c = 8, d = 2c, a + c + d = 12. Area of the square?$oq2$,$oq2$c = 16/5, d = 32/5, a = b = 12/5 → total 72/5 = 14.4 cm².$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (14,1,$oq2$A 60 cm² square stands beside a smaller square sharing a baseline. Triangle ABC joins the big square's top-left corner, the small square's top-right corner and the big square's bottom-left corner. Its area?$oq2$,$oq2$Both squares' diagonals make 45° with the base, so AC ∥ BD (D the shared corner). Triangles ABD and CBD share base BD and height, so they are equal; remove the common piece and the shaded triangle equals triangle ACD = half the big square = 30 cm².$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (14,2,$oq2$A right triangle with legs 20 and 30 contains a rectangle whose corners lie on the sides. Rectangle area?$oq2$,$oq2$Extend the rectangle's sides to build a congruent copy of the triangle on the other side; the pieces pair off (area a = area a'), which shows the rectangle has the same area as a 20 × 30 block → 600 cm².$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (14,3,$oq2$In a square box a yellow square (68 cm²) and blue square (17 cm²) sit in corners; a red square has one vertex at each of their centres. Red area?$oq2$,$oq2$68 = 4 × 17 so the box splits into 24 equal cells; yellow 16, blue 4, red 8 cells → 34 cm².$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (14,4,$oq2$Cut an 18-unit-square staircase shape into two 9-unit halves with one straight line.$oq2$,$oq2$Three ways: a vertical cut 2.25 from the left (height 4), a horizontal cut 1.8 up (width 5), or a slanted line through a corner (1 × 3 + ½ × 3 × (3 + x) = 9 → x = 1).$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (14,5,$oq2$In triangle ABC, D is on BC with AB = CD, ∠ABD = 48°, ∠BAD = 18°. Find ∠ACD.$oq2$,$oq2$Cut along AD and re-attach triangle ABD so AB lies on CD: ∠ADC = 66°, the new angle is 114°, and the rebuilt figure gives ∠ACD = 180 − 18 − 114 = 48°.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (15,1,$oq2$Alan: "Dylan broke it." Bob: "Dylan did." Charles: "I didn't." Dylan: "Bob is lying." Exactly one is truthful. Who did it?$oq2$,$oq2$If Dylan did it, Alan and Bob are both truthful — contradiction. Testing each: only Charles gives exactly one true statement (Dylan's).$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
insert into examples (chapter, n, prompt, solution) values (15,2,$oq2$A–E scored above 91; average of A, B, C is 95 and of B, C, D is 94; E = 96 is second highest; A is highest; B = D. Find D.$oq2$,$oq2$A − D = 3. A = 97 forces B = C = D = 94 (contradiction with B = D only? all equal is fine but B = C = D gives C = D — rejected by the book). A = 98 → D = 95, B = 95, C = 92 → D = 95.$oq2$) on conflict (chapter, n) do update set prompt = excluded.prompt, solution = excluded.solution;
